          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "**Please don't wait to see whether this settles.** Chest squeezing or heaviness that comes on with effort and eases with rest is a pattern that needs a medical assessment soon. The red-flag guidance says that chest discomfort, even when it eases, should not be left to see whether it passes. A symptom checker that found no emergency match here is not a clearance. Book an urgent appointment with a doctor or your local urgent service this week, and do not wait for a routine slot.\n\n**Call emergency services now (for example 911 or 999) if** the squeezing:\n- happens at rest, or with less effort than before\n- lasts more than a few minutes, or goes away and comes back\n- spreads to your arms, shoulders, neck, jaw, back or upper stomach\n- comes with breathlessness, cold sweat, nausea, vomiting, light-headedness, or a sense of impending doom\n\nThese guidance notes treat such symptoms as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise. (Source: *Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*, emergency medicine and cardiology society guidance.)\n\n**If it turns out to be angina, what the guidance describes (general, not personal advice):** The secondary-prevention guideline and the coronary artery disease summary describe several parts of care:\n- treating risk factors, such as smoking, blood pressure and cholesterol\n- antiplatelet and cholesterol-lowering medicines, and medicines to control angina symptoms; your clinician decides which, if any, apply to you\n- cardiac rehabilitation, a supervised exercise and education programme\n- stenting or bypass surgery, where symptoms persist despite medicines or the pattern of disease calls for it\n\nThe guideline also notes that taking medicines as prescribed often drops off in the year after a heart event, so regular follow-up matters. (Sources: *Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis*, major cardiology society consensus guidance; *Coronary artery disease*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**What these passages did not cover:** they did not say how angina is diagnosed or which tests you would have, and they did not give specific treatment choices or doses. Those questions are for your clinician.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
